Lesson {2} showed that (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b². Read backwards, this factorises any difference of two squares.
• {quad(1, 0, -64)} = x² − {8}² = {lin2(1, 8, 1, -8)}
• {quad(9, 0, -4)} = ({mono(3, 'x')})² − {2}² = {lin2(3, 2, 3, -2)}
Always look for a common factor first: {quad(5, 0, -45)} = {5}({quad(1, 0, -9)}) = {5}{lin2(1, 3, 1, -3)}.
A sum such as {quad(1, 0, 9)} has no factors of this kind.
